// Why the most recent attempt failed. `kind` is what a handler dispatches on,
// `message` is for a human, `code` is the adapter's own.
//
// Deliberately not `app.error`: that one is raised and unwinds to the nearest
// `errorBoundary`, whereas a failed fetch is a value a screen renders beside
// the stale data it still has. A program that wants the boundary behaviour
// raises an `app.error` from a `Failure` itself, which keeps the choice at the
// call site instead of in the adapter.
struct Failure {
    kind FailureKind
    message string
    // The adapter's own code, verbatim: an HTTP status, a SQLSTATE, a gRPC
    // status name, a GraphQL error code. Carried untouched because the only
    // code that can interpret it is code that knows which adapter produced it,
    // and folding it into `kind` would throw away exactly what that code
    // needs.
    code string
}

// The failure classes every adapter can produce.
//
// The split is by what the program should do next, because that is the only
// question a UI asks of a failure — which is why `request` and `server` are
// separate when both are "the peer said no": retrying a `request` fails
// identically, retrying a `server` may work.
//
//   - `transport` — never reached the peer
//   - `timeout` — reached it, no answer in time
//   - `auth` — a credential is missing, expired or rejected
//   - `request` — the peer rejected what was sent; retrying is pointless
//   - `server` — the peer failed on its own side; retrying may work
//   - `decode` — the answer arrived but did not match the declared type
//   - `cancelled` — superseded by a newer attempt, or the owner unmounted
//
// `cancelled` is a failure rather than a state of its own because a screen
// treats it as one: there is no value and no reason to wait. It is the kind
// that should not be shown to a user.
enum FailureKind { transport, timeout, auth, request, server, decode, cancelled }
